mCertiKOS - one project for all seven lab experiments
=====================================================

This single project carries all lab experiments of the course. You work on
it progressively: every lab builds on the code you wrote in the previous
labs, so keep your solutions and continue in the same tree.

  Lab 1: physical memory allocator          kern/pmm/MATIntro, MATInit, MATOp
  Lab 2: memory containers and page tables  kern/pmm/MContainer, kern/vmm/MPTIntro
  Lab 3: virtual memory management          kern/vmm/MPTOp, MPTComm, MPTKern, MPTNew
  Lab 4: kernel threads and scheduling      kern/thread/PKCtxNew, PTCBInit,
                                            PTQueueInit, PThread
  Lab 5: processes, traps, system calls     kern/trap/TSyscallArg, TSyscall,
                                            TTrapHandler
  Lab 6: multiprocessor and synchronization kern/trap/TTrapInit, kern/thread/PThread,
                                            kern/trap/TSyscall (bbuf.c, TSyscall.c)
  Lab 7: disk and file system               kern/trap/TTrapHandler, kern/fs/*

After the seven experiments, the course project is built with LAB=8 (see the
project description).

The lab sheets (objectives, tasks, expected output and evaluation) are in
the labs/ directory: labs/LAB1.md ... labs/LAB7.md.

Every task is marked in the code with a comment of the form

  TODO [Lab <n>]

To list the tasks of, e.g., lab 2:   grep -rn "TODO \[Lab 2\]" kern user
Tasks marked "(optional)" are not required. All tasks are in C; you do not
need to write any assembly code.

Requirements
------------
Install the tools with the setup script in the directory above this one
(bash ../setup.sh); see ../SETUP.md and Chapter 2 of the lab handout. The
project builds on Ubuntu/Debian, Fedora, macOS (i686-elf cross-compiler) and
Windows (WSL 2).

QEMU uses KVM automatically when /dev/kvm is available (much faster).

Selecting the lab
-----------------
The LAB variable tells the build which lab you are working on. It decides how
far the kernel boots and which tests run. Either change "LAB ?= 1" in the
Makefile when you move to the next lab, or pass it on the command line:

  make LAB=2 ...

The tree is rebuilt from scratch automatically whenever LAB or TEST changes.

Commands
--------
Compile:        make LAB=<n>
Run the tests:  make LAB=<n> TEST=1
                (builds, boots qemu and prints "LAB <n> RESULT: x / y test
                groups passed."; exit qemu with Ctrl-a x)
Run in qemu:    make LAB=<n> && make qemu-nox      (or: make qemu)
Debug with gdb: make qemu-gdb / make qemu-nox-gdb
                (in another terminal) gdb

List here the following info:
1. who you have worked with
2. whether you coded this assignment together, and if not, who worked on which part
3. brief description of what you have implemented
4. and anything else you would like us to know
